-- title:
--   Regular-differential half of the ordinary corner count at p
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime with $p\neq 2$ (`hp2`), $M$ is a nonzero natural number with $p\mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2\nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and $H\le(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ is a subgroup containing the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/M)^\times\to(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ (`hHp`). A set $S$ of natural numbers indexes the excluded primes of the generator type [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13), whose elements are $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q\mid M$, and diamonds $\langle d\rangle$ for $d\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$. The hypothesis `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L113): for every prime $\ell$ the correspondence inputs `HeckeInputsHAlong` hold for $(M,H,\ell)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and for every $d\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ there is an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra automorphism of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` which is a diamond automorphism for $d$. Here $J_H(M)$ denotes [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127), the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of that function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) is the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^nx_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$.
--
--   **Hecke algebra and ordinary corner.** $\mathbb T$ is a commutative ring which is a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra and acts on the Tate module compatibly with the $\mathbb Z_p$-structure, the action being faithful (`hfaith`). A map `op` sends each generator $g$ to an element of $\mathbb T$ acting on the Tate module as the endomorphism `tateGenOpH` induced by the Hecke or diamond operator `genOpH M H S g` (`hop`), and the $\mathbb Z_p$-subalgebra generated by the image of `op` is all of $\mathbb T$ (`hgen`). $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb T$: finitely many complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb T$ and satisfying $e_i\in\mathfrak m_j\iff i\neq j$; an index $i_0$ is fixed with $\mathrm{op}(U_p)\notin\mathfrak m_{i_0}$ (`hord`, the ordinarity of the chosen corner).
--
--   **The place and the inertia-fixed corner.** $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ in its nonunits (`hPl`), its residue field being algebraically closed of characteristic $p$. A $\mathbb T$-submodule $P_0$ of the Tate module is given together with `hP0`, which identifies its elements as those $x$ lying in `cornerSubmodule (S'.e i₀)`, the range of multiplication by $e_{i_0}$, and satisfying $\rho(\sigma)x=\chi_{\mathrm{cyc}}(\sigma)\,x$ for every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb Q$, where $\rho$ is the Galois action on the Tate module and $\chi_{\mathrm{cyc}}$ the $p$-adic cyclotomic character.
--
--   **Differentials in characteristic $p$.** $K$ is an algebraically closed field which is an algebra over $\mathbb Z/p$, and $\Gamma'=\mathrm{\Gamma}_{H'}(M/p)$ is [`CohCarrier.GammaH (M / p) (infSubgroup p M H hpM)`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133), with $H'$ the image of $H$ in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$. The space `ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p` consists of the Kähler differentials of the $q$-expansion function field `qExpFunctionFieldC K Γ'` over $K$ that are regular away from the supersingular places and have at worst simple poles there. A ring homomorphism $\tau$ from $\mathbb T$ to the $K$-linear endomorphisms of this space is given, with `hτ`: for each generator $g$, $\tau(\mathrm{op}(g))$ acts as `genDiffModL K p M H hpM S g`, that is as the Hecke correspondence operator for $T_\ell$ and for $U_q$ with $q\neq p$, as the Frobenius pushforward for $U_p$, and as the diamond operator attached to the image of $d$ in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ for $\langle d\rangle$.
--
--   **Integral model, level data and Néron object.** The hypothesis `hj` places the $q$-expansion of $j$ in the full-level function field over $\mathbb Q$; $\mathfrak X$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, a Deligne–Rapoport-style integral model package at $p$ for $X_H(M)$ over $R_p$, carrying among other data a curve model $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with its bijection `pointEquivPlace` between sections and places, the comparison isomorphism $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{eeta}$, cusp sections $\varepsilon_\infty$, the degeneracy morphisms $\pi,\pi_w$, the Atkin–Lehner isomorphism $\mathfrak X.w$, the diamond isomorphisms $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{dia0}$, a special-fibre curve model $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Mfib}$ with its comparison $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{efib}$ and components $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{comp}$, and a smooth locus. $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`: a section $\sigma_A$ of the base over $Pl$ lifting the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base with a relative group law, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_{H'}(M/p)$ to sections over the generic point, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ from $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the special-fibre function field to sections over the residue point. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from $(\Lambda.X,\Lambda.f)$ and the unit section represents the relative sub-Picard functor for the fibrewise-algebraically-trivial condition on the curve over $R_p$ of level $\Gamma_N$, rigidified by $\varepsilon_\infty$ followed by $\pi$. $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`: a smooth separated group scheme $O.G$ over the base with relative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ to its sections over the generic point, Hecke endomorphisms, a toric rank, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ of $J_H(M)$.
--
--   **Representability and Abel–Jacobi pins** (the hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ`, `hsep`, `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law`, `hAJ`, summarised here). They require: that $(O.G,O.g)$ with its unit section represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the model over $R_p$ and, after base change, over $\mathbb Q$, the latter base change being separated; that $\mathrm{aj}_{\mathbb Q}$ is a section over the base-changed model which is compatible with the zero section and whose pullback of the Poincaré bundle is, for every field-valued point, the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point tensored with the ideal module of the cusp divisor, so that $\mathrm{aj}_{\mathbb Q}$ is the Abel–Jacobi map $x\mapsto[x-\varepsilon_\infty]$; that $k_{\mathbb Q}$ is the comparison between the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$- and $\mathbb Q$-pullbacks of the model, compatible with both projections; that $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ is the composite of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{eeta}$, $k_{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathrm{aj}_{\mathbb Q}$ and the first projection and lies over the generic point; that $\bar\varepsilon$ is the cusp section and is carried by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ to the unit; that the Poincaré bundles of `hDQ` and of the base change of `hD` are isomorphic; that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law coming from `hD`; and that for any two sections $x,s$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}$ with $s$ the cusp, there is a degree-zero divisor equal to $(x)-(s)$ whose class is carried by $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   **The inertia ring.** $R$ is a henselian local domain with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with a faithful algebra structure over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ whose image lies in $Pl$ (`hRA`), which is a discrete valuation ring (`hRdvr`) in which $p$ is irreducible (`hRirr`), such that an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ lies in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ exactly when it fixes $R$ pointwise (`hRfix`), and every element of $Pl$ fixed by inertia comes from $R$ (`hRmax`).
--
--   **Finite part: the $p$-divisible group layer.** $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), given by finite free $R$-Hopf algebras `level v` of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals. An injective additive map $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`) carries the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $\mathcal G$ into $J_H(M)$, with: `hΔlev`, identifying $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ with the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points for every $v$; `hΔgal`, the equivariance of $\Delta$ for automorphisms of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ over $\mathbb Q$ that are $R$-linear; and `hΔhecke`, which for every generator $g$ produces a system of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, compatible with the transitions, inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$.
--
--   **Raynaud/toric splitting.** $\mathcal B$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) together with level maps $\psi_v:\mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v\to\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$, and natural numbers satisfying $h=O.\mathrm{toricRank}+h_B$ (`hhB`) and $h_B=2h'$ (`hhB2`). The hypotheses are: `hψt`, compatibility of $\psi$ with transitions; `hψker`, that a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal G$ becomes trivial on $\mathcal B$ exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; `hψsurj`, surjectivity on level-$v$ points; `hψred`, that if the $\mathcal B$-part of a point reduces to the counit (all values of $\mathrm{toAlgHom}$ minus the counit of $Pl$-valuation $<1$) then so does the point itself; and `hperiod`, that for $\sigma$ in inertia, a $p^v$-torsion class $z$ and a level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y)=\sigma\cdot z-z$, the $\mathcal B$-part of $y$ reduces to the counit.
--
--   **Embedding of the layer into the Néron object** (`ρh`, `ι`, `hρh`, `hιbase`, `hιcl`, `hιp`, `hιpts`, `hιmul`, `hιt`, `hιhecke`, `hιfin`). A ring map $\rho_h:R_p\to R$ compatible with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and morphisms $\iota_v:\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v\to O.G$ are given such that: $\iota_v$ lies over $\rho_h$ and induces a closed immersion into the fibre product; $\iota_v$ is killed by multiplication by $p^v$; $\iota_v$ computes $O.\mathrm{pts}\circ\Delta$ on points; $\iota_v$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law on points with values in any $R$-algebra; the $\iota_v$ are compatible with the transitions; every Hecke generator is realised by coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ compatible with transitions, with $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ through $\iota_v$ and with `genOpH` through $\Delta$; and the induced map $j_v$ into the base change along $\rho_h$ of the $p^v$-torsion of $O.G$ is an open and closed immersion whose image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner data.** $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, that is a pair of a ring automorphism of the function field and one of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ that are compatible via the structure map, pinned by `hwgen` to the model involution $\mathfrak X.w$ on places. Moreover $\theta$ is a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra automorphism of the same function field with `hθ`: whenever $f$ agrees as a Laurent series with an element $u$ of the level-$(M/p)$ function field, then $\theta f$ is the series $u$ with $q$ replaced by $q^{p}$ (`qExpand`); and `hwθ` states $w_{\mathrm{gen}}=\mathrm{ofAlgAut}(\theta)$.
--
--   **Specialisation pins.** A ring map $\rho:R_p\to Pl$ compatible with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρ`) is given with $\Lambda.\sigma_A=\operatorname{Spec}\rho$ (`hσA`). The hypothesis `hsp` (thirteen data and fourteen side conditions, summarised here) states: for $i\in\{0,1\}$, given two sections $y_1,y_2$ of $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}$ with lifts $u_1,u_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho$ meeting the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points and with image inside the smooth locus, given fibre sections $u_{\kappa,1},u_{\kappa,2}$ over the residue field compatible with the $u_j$, given closed points $P_1,P_2$ of the special-fibre model lying over them on the $i$-th component, a degree-zero divisor $D_v=(y_1)-(y_2)$ and an admissible gluing datum $x$ whose first divisor is $(P_1)-(P_2)$ if $i=0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second divisor is $(P_1)-(P_2)$ if $i=1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit part is $0$, there is a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}([D_v])$ equal to $s$ restricted to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and with the reduction of $s$ corresponding under $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ to the glued class of $x$. The hypothesis `hspΛ` is the analogous statement for $\Lambda$: with $Q_1,Q_2$ closed points lying over the images under $\pi$ (for $i=0$) or $\pi_w$ (otherwise), $D_v=(y_1)-(y_2)$ on the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-curve and $D_w=(Q_1)-(Q_2)$ on the special fibre, there is a section $s_0$ of $\Lambda.f$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,i\,[D_v])$ the restriction of $s_0$ and with reduction the class of $D_w$. The hypothesis `hdia0` states that transporting a closed point of the special-fibre model through $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{dia0}\,e$ acts on places as the diamond automorphism `diamondActionModL` attached to $e\in(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$.
--
--   **Special-fibre operators and degeneracies.** An element $d\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ is given whose image in $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ is $p$ (`hd`) and such that this image or its negative lies in $H'$ (`hdH`), together with $M/p\neq 0$. Additive endomorphisms $F,F^{-1},F^{*}$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the special-fibre function field are given with $F$ the $q$-expansion Frobenius pushforward at level $\Gamma_N$ (`hF`), $F^{-1}$ a two-sided inverse of $F$ (`hFinv`), and $F^{*}=p\cdot F^{-1}$ (`hFstar`). An element $\bar p\in(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ reducing to $p$ (`hpb`) determines the diamond endomorphism $\delta$ of the same group (`hδ`). Two degeneracy homomorphisms $\alpha_i:J_{H'}(M/p)\to J_H(M)$ and two sections $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.f$ are given with: `hpull`, that $O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_i x)$ is $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\mathrm{degPull}_i$; `hpullsp`, that on reductions the pair of classes attached to $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is $(z,F^{*}z)$ for $i=0$ and $(F^{*}z,\delta z)$ for $i=1$; and `hpull_mul`, that each $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is compatible with the relative group laws. Finally $\overline W$ is the endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ given by the action of $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ (`hWbar`), and `hUPgen` asserts the relation $\mathrm{genOpH}(U_p)x+\overline Wx=\alpha_1(O.\mathrm{degPts}\,0\,x)$ for all $x$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Write $e=S'.e\,i_0$, let $\Omega_{\mathrm{reg}}$ be the preimage in `ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p` of the submodule of everywhere-regular differentials of `qExpFunctionFieldC K Γ'` over $K$, and set
--   $$\mathfrak d=\dim_K\bigl(\tau(e)\,\Omega_{\mathrm{reg}}\bigr),\qquad X=\mathrm{proj}_1\bigl(e\cdot\mathrm{T}_p J_H(M)\bigr)\subseteq J_H(M),$$
--   where $\mathrm{proj}_1$ is the component at index $1$ of the Tate module and $e\cdot\mathrm{T}_pJ_H(M)$ is `cornerSubmodule e` viewed as an additive subgroup. Then
--   $$p^{\mathfrak d}\cdot \#\{x\in X:\ \exists\,y\in\mathcal G.\mathrm{Point}(\overline{\mathbb Q})_1,\ \Delta(y)=x\ \text{and}\ \forall a\in\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,1,\ \mathrm{val}_{Pl}\bigl(y(a)-\varepsilon(a)\bigr)<1\}\;=\;\#\{x\in X:\ x\in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)\},$$
--   both cardinalities being `Set.ncard`, where $y(a)$ denotes the value at $a$ of the $R$-algebra map attached to the level-$1$ point $y$, $\varepsilon$ the counit followed by $R\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and $\Delta(y)$ the image under $\Delta$ of the class of $y$ in the direct limit of level points; thus the left-hand set consists of the points of the corner at level $1$ that come from layer points reducing to the identity, and the right-hand set of those lying in the finite part.
--
--   This is the counting step that compares, inside the $p$-torsion of the ordinary corner $e\cdot\mathrm{T}_pJ_H(M)$, the points coming from the connected part of the $p$-divisible group layer with those in the whole finite part, the index being $p$ raised to the dimension of the $e$-part of the regular differentials in characteristic $p$; the exponent enters through the logarithmic derivative on the special fibre. It is used by the two statements that turn this comparison into the count of the full corner and into the count of the $e$-part of the regular differentials.
